## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests end-to-end system thinking across multilingual NLP, streaming latency, privacy-by-design, RAG, and deployment.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual intent classifier with adapters trained on English/Spanish/Japanese corpora
- Streaming ASR integration and latency budgets (target ~150 ms)
- Privacy: data minimization, on-device processing where possible, PII masking, tenant isolation
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation for consistent responses
- Deployment: feature flags, drift monitoring, rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code illustrating a multilingual intent classifier with adapters
const { Tokenizer, Model } = require('transformers-js');
const tokenizer = Tokenizer.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base');
const model = Model.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base', { numLabels: 50 });
model.addAdapter('multilingual');
model.setActiveAdapters(['multilingual']);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor model drift across languages and handle changes in intents?
- How would you design a safe fallback path when the classifier has low confidence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device multilingual NLP for real-time support escalation, balancing simplicity with practical constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **On-device inference**: Use TensorFlow Lite or ONNX Runtime Mobile for sub-100ms inference
- **Multilingual code-switch handling**: Character n-grams are language-agnostic; use Unicode normalization for emoji
- **Hybrid rule+ML approach**: Rules catch obvious cases (fast path), ML handles ambiguity
- **Latency targets**: 120ms p99 includes preprocessing + inference + postprocessing
- **Privacy**: On-device only, no data transmission, local differential privacy for any updates
- **Evaluation**: Offline metrics followed by staged rollout with careful monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// On-device scoring with TF-Lite
function scoreEscalation(messages, tfliteInterpreter) {
  const input = preprocessMessages(messages); // normalize, tokenize
  tfliteInterpreter.setInput(input);
  tfliteInterpreter.invoke();
  const output = tfliteInterpreter.output(0);
  return { score: output[0], threshold: 0.7 }; // escalate if above threshold
}

function checkRules(messages) {
  const urgentPatterns = [/\bhelp\b/i, /\burgent\b/i, /speak.*human/i];
  const urgentEmojis = ['🚨', '🙋', '😡', '😭'];
  const text = messages.join(' ');
  if (urgentPatterns.some(p => p.test(text)) || urgentEmojis.some(e => text.includes(e))) {
    return { escalate: true, confidence: 0.95, source: 'rule' };
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to drift or new escalation cues?
  - Use online learning with local batch updates, or periodic model refresh via federated learning
- How to measure impact on support workload and false positives?
  - Track: tickets deflected, false positive rate, escalation accuracy, user satisfaction scores

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a grounded, scalable NLP system with source provenance, strict latency requirements, and multilingual support—critical skills for production AI systems in enterprise environments.

## Key Concepts
- RAG architectures, vector stores, and source citations
- Multilingual routing and language detection
- Latency budgets, caching strategies, and approximate nearest neighbor search optimizations
- Compliance: provenance tracking, human escalation triggers, and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Routing and retrieval pipeline
const detectLanguage = (query) => langdetect.detect(query)[0].lang;

const routeQuery = async (query) => {
  const lang = detectLanguage(query);
  const embedding = await embedQuery(query);
  const results = await vectorStore.search(embedding, { k: 5, filter: { lang } });
  const citations = results.map(r => ({ source: r.metadata.source, text: r.text }));

  if (results.some(r => r.metadata.confidence < 0.7)) {
    return { status: 'escalate', citations };
  }

  return { status: 'respond', citations, context: results };
};
```
